<h2>Section: 2 – Case study details</h2>

<p class="q">Q.1. Describe the problem or challenge your solution seeks to address. Why is this problem important for India, and who is affected by it? (300 words limit)</p>
<p>India has started pricing carbon, and the clock is already running. From FY 2025-26, around 490 large industrial plants across seven sectors carry legally binding emission-intensity targets under the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme. Their first compliance filing is due on 31 July 2026, and the penalty for a shortfall is twice the market price of a carbon credit. On the export side, Europe's CBAM moved into its paid phase on 1 January 2026. Roughly €8–9 billion of Indian exports now carry a carbon cost, and GTRI expects coal-based steel and aluminium to lose 15–22% in effective price terms.</p>
<p>Big companies will manage. The pressure lands on their suppliers, who cannot. India has 8.7 crore Udyam-registered MSMEs. Together they produce 31.1% of GDP, account for 48.6% of exports, and employ 38.9 crore people. Almost none of them can tell you their carbon footprint. A consultant engagement costs more than most small units spend on all their paperwork in a year. The software large companies use is priced for large companies, takes months to set up, and speaks only English. Meanwhile, a tile maker in Morbi keeps his records as paper bills and photos on a phone. He has no sustainability team. He has himself.</p>
<p>So two things go wrong at once. Export-linked units start losing orders because they cannot produce the emissions data their buyers now demand. And the country cannot reach its NDC promise of cutting emission intensity by 45% by 2030 while a third of the economy stays unmeasured. The people affected are millions of small factory owners and the 38.9 crore workers who depend on them. You cannot manage what you cannot measure, and today most of India's industrial base cannot measure.</p>

<p class="q">Q.2. Describe your idea (design thinking approach, community-led model, product or process innovation, etc). How is your idea different, more practical, or more inclusive than existing solutions, and how does it support India's sustainability, resilience or net-zero ambitions? (Max 300 words)</p>
<p>CarbonSathi is a carbon accounting and compliance assistant for small factories. The owner photographs an electricity bill or a fuel invoice, in whatever language it comes in, and the platform does the rest.</p>
<p>Behind that simple front are four steps. AI reads the uploaded document and pulls out the consumption data. An India-specific calculation engine applies the right factors, including the current CEA grid factor of 0.7117 tCO₂/MWh plus IPCC and India GHG Program values, to compute Scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions. The owner gets a live dashboard and reports shaped for CCTS-style compliance and for buyers asking CBAM questions. The system then suggests practical savings ranked by payback period, tracks water use and material waste, and shows how ready the unit is to earn carbon credits.</p>
<p>I designed it around how small units actually work. Records live on paper and WhatsApp. Nobody has time to learn carbon accounting, so the software absorbs that burden instead.</p>
<p>The existing options all miss this market. Persefoni and Watershed are built and priced for large corporates. Indian tools like sentra.world, Greenio and Zoei still assume a sustainability officer who works in English. Consultants do good work but cannot serve lakhs of units continuously. Nobody combines AI document reading, Indian languages, India-specific factors, compliance-ready output and a price of a few hundred rupees a month. That combination is the idea.</p>
<p>It is also the inclusive path to net zero. Measurement is the first step to reduction. If crores of small businesses can finally measure, India's carbon market reaches the base of the economy, where the NDC targets will actually be won.</p>

<p class="q">Q.3. Explain how your solution contributes to the selected challenge theme. (Max 300 words)</p>
<p>CarbonSathi builds the measurement, reporting and verification layer that India's smallest factories currently lack — the foundation of carbon management.</p>
<p>Clean energy: the recommendation engine finds electricity-heavy processes and shows the payback on rooftop solar and efficient motors. BEE has proven this approach, facilitating 800 kWp of rooftop solar across 18 ceramic plants in Thangadh, with typical paybacks under three and a half years. My platform turns that kind of one-off programme into a standing recommendation every unit sees.</p>
<p>Resource efficiency: dashboards show energy, water and material use per unit of production. BEE's SME audits in Morbi and Tiruppur repeatedly found double-digit savings potential. An audit happens once; software watches every month.</p>
<p>Climate resilience: a unit that can produce emissions data keeps its buyers, avoids penalties and stays in the supply chain. That is resilience in its most practical form.</p>
<p>Sustainable infrastructure: over time I want the factor engine and methodology to become open rails others can build on, the way UPI and ONDC opened payments and commerce.</p>
<p>Environmental benefits: the first-year pilot targets about 35,000 tCO₂e measured and 2,500 tCO₂e cut annually across 200 units on conservative assumptions, alongside roughly 2.2 GWh of enabled solar generation and over 100 million litres of water saved in textile units.</p>
<p>Social benefits: the MSME sector employs 38.9 crore people, and protecting export orders protects those jobs. The pilot also trains local youth as "Carbon Mitras" and onboards women-led units first.</p>
<p>Economic benefits: 5–15% energy savings per unit, easier access to green finance, and future credit revenue once carbon trading opens to smaller players.</p>
<p>Scale: cluster by cluster, then state, then national, pulled along by CCTS growing toward more than 740 obligated entities whose suppliers will all need this data.</p>

<p class="q">Q.4. Mention the current development stage of your solution. Also, is it tested or validated? (300 words limit)</p>
<p>o Idea Stage&nbsp;&nbsp; o Proof of concept&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="hl">o Functional Prototype</span>&nbsp;&nbsp; o MVP&nbsp;&nbsp; o Simulation&nbsp;&nbsp; o Pilot-Tested Solution&nbsp;&nbsp; o Market-Ready</p>
<p>The platform runs today. It has a React frontend, Node.js APIs, MongoDB, and login secured with JWT and OAuth. The calculation engine carries the current CEA grid factor (0.7117 tCO₂/MWh, database version 21.0) along with IPCC and India GHG Program fuel factors. The AI module reads uploaded electricity bills and fuel invoices and turns them into structured consumption data. There is a live dashboard and there are PDF reports that mirror the data fields of a CCTS Form A.</p>
<p>On validation: I cross-checked the engine's output against published CEA and BEE reference values, and tested the AI extraction on simulated datasets built around two typical units, a ceramic kiln and a textile dyeing house. The full flow works end to end, from upload to finished report.</p>
<p>What I have not done yet, and will not pretend otherwise: there is no live commercial pilot, no integration with accredited verifiers or the carbon market portal, and language coverage is still partial. The next step is a 90-day pilot with 15–20 units through a cluster association in Morbi or Tiruppur, turning simulated validation into field evidence.</p>
<p>Why believe I can execute: I have already shipped two production platforms on this exact stack — a real-time collaborative coding and interview product with AI evaluation built in, and a fault-tolerant webhook security service that survived concurrent load without losing a single payload. Real-time dashboards, AI pipelines and secure multi-tenant systems are what I have actually built, and they are precisely what CarbonSathi needs.</p>

<p class="q">Q5. What is innovative about your solution? (existing alternatives compared; effectiveness, affordability, inclusivity; most novel aspect; cost/efficiency/impact)</p>
<p>What exists today, honestly compared: global platforms like Persefoni, Watershed and Sweep are excellent for corporates and priced accordingly, with long deployments and no CCTS awareness. Indian tools such as sentra.world, Greenio and Zoei ESG sit closer to our market but still assume an English-speaking sustainability person on staff. IIM Bangalore offers a free calculator, but it needs manual entry and produces no compliance outputs. Consultants are credible but expensive per engagement and cannot cover lakhs of units continuously. The real status quo is Excel, or nothing.</p>
<p>Why mine works where these do not: the biggest barrier is not awareness, it is data entry. A factory owner will not type bills into a form. So the AI reads the bills instead. Photograph, upload, done. On cost: a few hundred rupees a month against consultant engagements costing orders of magnitude more, roughly a hundred times cheaper per measured unit. On reach: a vernacular, mobile-first product gets to owners no English software will ever touch.</p>
<p>The most novel piece is the pipeline itself: AI tuned to Indian documents, from state DISCOM bill formats to informal fuel invoices in regional languages, feeding straight into CCTS- and CBAM-shaped outputs, at a price a small unit can pay. Nobody occupies that quadrant today.</p>
<p>Does it cut cost, improve efficiency, increase impact? All three. Cost falls about 100× versus the consultant route. Measurement time drops from weeks to minutes. And a segment producing roughly 31% of GDP becomes visible to India's carbon system for the first time.</p>

<p class="q">Q.6. Briefly mention the exact outcome of your solution that aims to achieve:</p>
<p><i>CarbonSathi launches with the pilot, so there is no historical data to report; the year columns are left blank. The last column shows first-year pilot targets, each with the basis behind the number.</i></p>
