<tr><td>Annual CO₂ reduction (tCO₂e)</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>~2,500 tCO₂e/yr cut and ~35,000 tCO₂e/yr measured across 200 pilot units (basis: CEA grid factor 0.7117 tCO₂/MWh; BEE SME audit savings rates)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Energy saved</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>~3.5 GWh/yr in aggregate; 5–15% per unit (basis: BEE SME cluster audits, Morbi and Tiruppur manuals)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Renewable energy generated</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>~2.2 GWh/yr enabled through platform-recommended rooftop solar in ~20 pilot units of ~75 kW each (basis: BEE facilitated 800 kWp across 18 ceramic plants in Thangadh; paybacks of 2.8–3.4 years at ₹45,000/kW)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water conserved</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>100–150 million litres/yr across textile pilot units through water-energy tracking (basis: dyeing consumes ~200 litres per kg of fabric; Tiruppur halved its water use in a decade, per CII)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Waste diverted from landfill</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>~2,000 tonnes/yr of fired ceramic waste and sludge diverted through material-flow matching (basis: ceramic waste runs 15–30% of production; Morbi is India's largest tile hub)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Circular materials recovered</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>~1,000 tonnes/yr returned to tile production as substitute raw material (basis: published studies validate 10–20% sludge substitution in tile bodies)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost savings</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>₹4 crore+ combined annual energy savings (about ₹2 lakh per unit); solar adopters cut electricity bills 50–70%; the platform costs an MSME ₹500–2,000 a month</td></tr>
<tr><td>Productivity improvement</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>Emissions measurement per unit falls from weeks of consulting to minutes of upload; every pilot unit becomes buyer/CBAM-report ready</td></tr>
</table>

<p class="q">Q.6.1. Explain the most significant impact created or expected — environmental, social and economic aspects (Max 150 words)</p>
<p>Environmental: the pilot alone should bring about 35,000 tCO₂e under measurement and cut around 2,500 tCO₂e a year, alongside 2.2 GWh of enabled solar generation and over 100 million litres of water saved. At national scale this becomes millions of tonnes, feeding directly into the NDC's 45% intensity target.</p>
<p>Social: the MSME sector employs 38.9 crore people. Keeping small exporters inside carbon-conscious supply chains protects those livelihoods. The pilot trains local youth as Carbon Mitras and brings women-led units in first.</p>
<p>Economic: 5–15% off energy bills per unit, export orders preserved within €8–9 billion of CBAM-exposed trade, and a route to credit revenue once CCC trading begins, expected around October 2026 with early price estimates of ₹250–1,500 per tonne.</p>

<p class="q">Q.7. Have you collaborated or partnered with any NGOs, government bodies, startups, corporates, or academic institutions in supporting your solution? If yes, describe the nature and contribution. (Max 300 words)</p>
<p>Not yet, and I would rather say that plainly than dress it up. I am an individual applicant a few months out of college. What I do have is a specific plan for who I need and why.</p>
<p>Cluster associations come first: the Morbi ceramic manufacturers' association and the Tiruppur exporters' association, because they are the trusted door to hundreds of units. My first 30 days after this round are reserved for securing pilot letters of intent from them. District MSME facilitation offices come next, for official introductions and linkage to schemes like RAMP. BEE's SME programme matters for methodology: my factor engine follows their published cluster manuals so the numbers stand up to scrutiny. IIIT Vadodara, my alma mater, offers technical mentorship and student support for field work. Climate incubators such as Climate Collective are the route to funding and ecosystem access, and those applications are going out in parallel with this one.</p>
<p>Further ahead, accredited verifiers become integration partners, using the platform as their field-data layer.</p>
<p>If this challenge gives me one thing beyond validation, I hope it is the credibility to convert this pipeline into signed pilots faster.</p>

<p class="q">Q.8. Explain the sustainability, scalability, replicability and inclusivity plan of the solution and mention the steps taken to make it inclusive and replicable in the future* (300 words limit)</p>
<p>Money: freemium to start, then paid tiers between ₹500 and ₹2,000 a month for compliance reports and recommendations, then association-level licences where one contract covers hundreds of units, and eventually a small fee on the verification marketplace. Costs stay low because the product is cloud-native and AI does the work a consultant would.</p>
<p>Scale: the architecture is stateless and queue-based, patterns I have already run in production, so it scales horizontally without redesign. Distribution scales the same way: one association partnership onboards a whole cluster, keeping customer acquisition cost close to zero. Demand compounds on its own, because CCTS is heading toward more than 740 obligated entities whose suppliers will all be asked for emissions data.</p>
<p>Replication: the factor engine is configuration-driven. Adding a new sector or state means a new configuration, not a rebuild. I plan to publish the methodology openly so anyone can audit or adapt it. The long-term ambition is ONDC-style open rails for carbon data.</p>
<p>Inclusion, in concrete steps: an interface being built in 10+ Indian languages with voice guidance, mobile-first design that works on weak connections, onboarding that assumes zero prior knowledge, a hard price floor so the smallest units are never priced out, discounted tiers and first-priority onboarding for women-led MSMEs, and Carbon Mitra training that recruits local youth, including from ITIs.</p>
<p>The impact sustains itself because the incentives line up. The platform earns from measurement while the savings go to the factory. The greener and more efficient a unit becomes, the more useful the product is to it. Growth and environmental outcome reinforce each other instead of trading off.</p>

<p class="q">Q.9. Elevator pitch: Explain in a few lines why your idea deserves to be selected as a winner. Highlight what makes it unique, impactful, and scalable. (300 words limit)</p>
<p>Two dates changed the rules for Indian industry. On 1 January 2026, Europe started charging for the carbon in our exports. On 31 July 2026, India's own first carbon-compliance filings fall due. Between those dates, measuring carbon stopped being optional. Yet 8.7 crore MSMEs, producing 31% of our GDP, still cannot measure a single tonne.</p>
<p>My answer is simple: stop asking small factories to learn carbon accounting, and let AI do it. Photograph a bill in any Indian language and get Scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions, compliance-ready reports and ranked savings actions in minutes, for a few hundred rupees a month.</p>
<p>No global platform, Indian tool or consultant offers AI document reading, vernacular access, India-native factors and compliance output together at this price. The prototype already runs end to end, and I have shipped production AI platforms before this one.</p>
<p>Every measured MSME moves India closer to its 45% intensity cut and to net zero 2070. We cannot manage what six crore businesses cannot measure. CarbonSathi makes them count.</p>

